{"name":"Melissa","hex":"#b5d96b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Melissa","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A taxonomic genus within the family Lamiaceae – lemon balm."]},{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Bee-nymph in Greek mythology.","A female given name from Ancient Greek, popular in the latter half of the 20th century."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Melissa"}],"entries":[{"title":"Melissa","blocks":[{"text":"Melissa is a feminine given name. The name comes from the Greek word μέλισσα (mélissa), \"bee\", which in turn comes from μέλι (meli), \"honey\". In Hittite, melit signifies \"honey\". Melissa is a common variant form, with others being Malissa, Melesa, Melessa, Meliza, Mellisa, Melosa, and Molissa."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Melissa","wikidata":"Q830357"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/John_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg/500px-John_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/John_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg/1280px-John_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/John_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg/500px-John_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"John William Godward, Melissa, 1898","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AJohn_William_Godward_-_Melissa%2C_1898.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/DossoDossi.jpg/500px-DossoDossi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/59/DossoDossi.jpg/1280px-DossoDossi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=